  "family": "Pinaceae",
  "memo": "The Douglas fir is North America's most important timber producer and one of the world's most impressive trees. It grows throughout the western U.S., from Central California to British Columbia. In California, it covers the Klamath, North Coast Ranges, Cascade, and the northern Sierra Nevada. This long-lived conifer is surpassed in height only by the coast redwood (Sequoia sempervirens); the tallest known is over 325 feet. The spirally arranged needles twist where they attach, causing them to lay flat along two sides of the branchlets.",
